18.13.m:
From limb to limb of hers
lay down consumption!
May it not reach the earth, nor the gods;
may it not reach heaven, nor the broad atmosphere;
may it not reach the waters, this dirt, O Agni;
may it not reach Yama and all the fathers!

I bind you with the milk of the earth;
I bind you with the milk of the plants;
I bind you with offspring, with wealth;
so bound, win this prize!

18.14.m:
I am he, you are she;
I am heaven, you are earth;
I am mind, you are speech.
Let the two of us unite here;
let us generate offspring!

18.15.m:
The Ruddy One engendered Heaven and Earth;
that thread Parameṣṭhin stretched out.
There Aja Ekapād found support;
he made firm Heaven and Earth with strength.

The Ruddy One made firm Heaven and Earth;
by him heaven was propped up, by him the vault;
by him the atmosphere and the spaces were measured out;
by him the gods found immortality.

The clans that came into being from your austerity,
following the calf and the gāyatrī, they have come here;
may they enter with an auspicious mind;
the calf, the Ruddy One, comes toward having the same mother.

18.16.m:
The Ruddy One is the begetter and the mouth of the sacrifice;
to the Ruddy One I sacrifice with speech, ear, and mind.
To the Ruddy One the gods go, being well-disposed;
may he make me ascend by ascents to communion.

The Ruddy One arranged the sacrifice for Viśvakarman;
from him these splendors have come unto me.
May I proclaim your navel at the center of the world.

He clothes himself in the womb of the earth;
he clothes himself in heaven, he in the atmosphere;
he has reached the summit of the sun,
the heavenly realms.

18.17.m:
This is the seat of Rohiṇī and the Ruddy One;
that is the path by which the dappled mare goes.
The Gandharvas, the Kaśyapas lead her up;
the poets guard her without heedlessness.

The Ruddy One climbed heaven
from the great flood;
the Ruddy One climbed all ascents.

Measure out the milk-rich, ghee-flowing [spoon];
this is the unhindered cow of the gods.
Let Indra drink soma; let there be safety;
O Agni, push away the foes!

18.18.m:
The calf of Virāj, the bull of thoughts,
white-backed, has mounted the atmosphere;
with ghee they praise the calf, the sun-ray;
being sacred formulation, let them magnify [him] with the sacred formulation!

Mount the sky, and mount the earth;
mount royalty, and mount wealth;
mount offspring, and mount immortality;
unite your body with the Ruddy One!

The sacrifices purified by sacred formulation bear you;
travelling the path, the bay horses bear you;
across the ocean, beyond the surging flood you shine.

The god worships the god;
he moves within the surging flood.
They kindle the common fire;
that the sages beyond know.

18.19.m:
Mounting the sky, immortal, protect my speech!
The sacrifices purified by sacred formulation bear you;
drinking ghee, the bay steeds bear you.

Broad were the enclosing sticks;
the earth was prepared as the altar;
there the Ruddy One placed these two fires:
the cold and the heat.

Of the heaven-finding Ruddy One,
fire was put together through sacred formulation;
from it the heat, from it the cold,
from it sacrifice was born.

One was placed in the waters;
the other was placed in truth;
[…]

18.20.m:
Rain was the ghee, heat the fire;
the earth was prepared as the altar;
there, with songs, Fire made
these mountains stand upright.

That sacrifice was the first to come to be;
it was born as what is to be;
from that sacrifice, indeed, everything,
whatever shines forth here,
was brought forth by the sage Ruddy One.

18.21.m:
Upward with his lofty banner the god has come;
he has uncovered the darkness, he has resorted to light;
the heavenly bird, powerful, has surveyed all the worlds,
the son of Aditi.

Rising, you stretch out your rays;
you look out upon all creatures;
with your wisdom you shine across both oceans,
encompassing all the realms, shining forth.

These two children, by creative power, move from east to west,
playing, they go around the surging flood;
the one clothes himself in all the worlds;
the bay horses carry [the other] with golden [trappings].

18.22.m:
Crossing, visible to all,
you are the maker of light, O Sūrya;
shining, you illumine all.

Sūrya has yoked the seven bright ones,
the daughters of the chariot;
with them, self-yoked, he journeys.

18.23.m:
The Ruddy One strode up to heaven by austerity, practicing austerity;
he goes to the womb, and he is born again;
he became the overlord of the gods.

He strode out further than the two-footed;
the two-footed comes up behind the three-footed;
the three-footed strode out further than the six-footed;
these sit together in the body of the one-footed.

Truly great are you, O Sūrya;
truly, O Āditya, great are you;
great is the greatness of you, the great one;
you, O Āditya, are great.

You shine in heaven, you shine in the intermediate space, O bird;
you shine on earth, you shine within the waters;
you have pervaded both oceans with radiance;
a god among gods are you, a buffalo, finding the light.

18.24.m:
Running upon the back of heaven, the fair-winged bird,
the son of Aditi, I approach in fear, seeking help;
Sūrya, extend our long life;
may we not suffer harm; may we be in your benevolence.

The Ruddy One became the world;
the Ruddy One was Prajāpati in the beginning;
the Ruddy One is the face of sacrifices;
the Ruddy One is called light.

The Ruddy One became the past;
the Ruddy One brought forth the future;
the Ruddy One is the face of sacrifices;
the Ruddy One brought forth the light of heaven.

The Ruddy One became Time;
the Ruddy One burned intensely over heaven;
the Ruddy One shall move with his rays
along the earth, along the ocean.

18.25.m:
He bears the ray; he puts on the garland,
his rays upward, the great ray;
the day-maker sees what is higher than the highest;
in the morning, by his height he filled all,
the golden, bay-hued banner, rising up.

Whose courses are three, though he is single,
to whom the races of gods bring tribute;
his is that heaven, the earth, and intermediate space;
they stand hidden, anointed with wealth.

18.26.m:
One of them clothes this earth;
one has encompassed the intermediate space;
he among them who is the upholder sustains heaven;
one of them protects all the directions.

18.27.m:
That became one; that became adorned;
that became great; that became the eldest;
that became austerity; that became truth;
that became the brahman; through that he propagated.

He grew; he became great;
he became Mahādeva; he became the Ruler;
he attained the rulership of the gods;
he became the sole Vrātya of the gods.

18.28.m:
In his southern direction:
[…] the mantra the Māgadha […];
day and [night] the two outrunners […].

In his western direction:
Refreshing drink is the harlot, laughter the Māgadha […];
full moon and new moon are the two outrunners […].

18.37.m:
Before the offering of the agnihotra, he should mutter:
"Vrātya, as it is dear to thee, so let it be;
Vrātya, as thy wish is, so let it be;
Vrātya, as thy will is, so let it be."

When he says: "Vrātya, as it is dear to thee, so let it be",
thereby he secures what is dear; he has what is dear in what is dear;
what is dear comes to him, who knows thus.

[When he says: "Vrātya, as] thy wish is, so let it be", thereby he secures wish;
he has wish in wish; wish comes to him, [who knows thus].
When he says: "Vrātya, as thy will is, so let it be",
thereby he secures will; he becomes ruler of will;
will comes to him, who knows thus.

18.46.m:
May Ruja and Vena not abandon me;
may Urva and Camasa not abandon me;
and may Vimoka […] not abandon me;
and may […] and Mātariśvan not abandon me;
and may Mūrdhan and Vidharman not abandon me;
and may the Upholder and Support not abandon me;
and may Mṛdha and Vaimṛdha not abandon me.

18.60.m:
Friends, we pray
with a prayer to Indra, wielder of the thunderbolt;
I praise the most manly, the bold one.

For by your might
you are the slayer of Vṛtra;
with bounteous gifts you surpass the liberal donor.

18.61.m:
Those who seek the gods invoke Sarasvatī;
Sarasvatī while the sacrifice is being extended.
Those who have done good deeds called upon Sarasvatī;
may Sarasvatī grant desirable treasure to the worshipper.

May the lower ones rise up, and the higher;
may the middle ancestors, worthy of Soma, rise up;
they who, harmless, knowers of cosmic order, have attained life-spirit—
may those ancestors help us in our invocations.

18.63.m:
Let your eye go to the sun,
your life-breath to the wind; and go to heaven
and to earth according to cosmic law;
or go to the waters, if that is suitable for you there;
stand firm in the plants with your limbs.

18.65.m:
Be pleasant to him,
thornless, offering a resting-place;
grant him broad protection.

Let not your mind, nor your spirit-breath,
nor your delight [be lost];
let not anything of your body be left behind.

Away from their houses the living have barred this one;
[send] him forth from the village here.
You are the wise messenger of Yama;
you have caused the spirits to go to the ancestors.

18.66.m:
Yama is beyond, lower is Vivasvant;
beyond that I do not look out upon anything else.
Upon Yama my sacrifice is set;
Vivasvant has extended along the world.

They hid the immortal [woman] from mortals;
having made one having fine wings, they gave her to Vivasvant.
And she bore the two Aśvins when that happened;
and Saraṇyū abandoned the two twins.

Heat auspiciously, do not over-heat;
O Agni, do not scorch the body.
Let your strength be [propitious];
what is on the earth let be […]

18.67.m:
Watery is the lowest heaven;
[such is] the middle one;
the third is indeed called the highest heaven,
in which the ancestors sit.

18.68.m:
Let Pūṣan, wise, [lead] you from here,
he who loses no cattle, the guardian of the world;
let him deliver you to these ancestors,
and Agni to the gods who find good.

This garment for you is the first […];
cast off that which [you wore] before.
Stride following the sacrifices and good deeds, knowing;
where they go in many ways, thither go.

18.82.m:
The moon runs within the waters;
the well-winged one in heaven.
Golden-rimmed lightnings,
they find not your track;
know this of mine, o Heaven and Earth!

19.1.m:
Sing at evening, sing high,
sing resplendently, o Atharvan;
praise god Savitṛ.

Press Soma for the Soma-drinker,
for Indra wielder of the vajra,
the young conqueror, the ruler, the much-praised.

Far from us let it be,
the missile, o gods, let it be far;
far away the slander of mortals!

Like a friend may Bounty be to us,
a friend Indra, a friend Savitṛ;
may Bhaga whose law is truth be our friend.

Protect us, o Indra-Pūṣan,
Varuṇa, may the Maruts protect;
o Apāṃ Napāt, seven rivers, protect [us];
may Viṣṇu and Heaven protect.

May Heaven and Earth protect us for our quest;
may the pressing-stone protect, may Soma protect us from distress;
may divine Sarasvatī, easy to invoke, protect us;
may Agni protect, and whatever guardians [there are].

May the two divine Aśvins of wondrous deeds protect us;
may Dawn and Night rescue us;
o Apāṃ Napāt, without deviation from anyone,
o god at the summit, o Aditi, grant us protection!

19.2.m:
O god, make [us] prosper for complete well-being,
and help us toward thought and glory;
rescue us;
o Father Heaven, keep misfortune away from here!

By the foot let trackers lead them;
let the fathers strike them with deadly blows,
so that not one of them may live.

Let evil go back to the evil-doer, o gods;
let sin go back, discerning, to the sinner;
let the knowing son go back to the father;
and let them destroy whatever is his own.

19.3.m:
Constantly may the lightning being made
fall turning backward;
may it fall back upon the doer.

19.4.m:
He spread out this [earth],
raising the light of the sacrifice;
at his will he releases the seasons.

With honey the rivers were filled,
the mountains and hills are honey;
honey is Paruṣṇī and Śīpālā;
let it be wellbeing for the mouth, wellbeing for the heart!

Let the waters, accustomed [and] fit for the ritual work,
release from here for the leading forward;
let them at once be ready to go!

In the impulse of the god Savitṛ
mortals perform their work;
may these waters and plants be wellbeing for us!

Around you the sandy shores
have stood across;
stand still, keep quiet, surely!

Stand still, lowest one; stand still, upper one;
and you, middle one, stand still!
Even the smallest one shall stand still;
the great channel indeed shall stand still.
